 "limits": "Absolute rates and effect sizes are not representative: Criteo sub-sampled the data non-uniformly so that the true incrementality level cannot be deduced. Use relative structure (ranking, heterogeneity, the correlation of baseline propensity and effect) and re-base with transport() to the world's own rates. The data pool several incrementality tests of undisclosed advertisers, countries and dates; the population is users reachable by Criteo's display advertising, not a general population. Features f0..f11 are anonymised random projections with no simulator counterpart; the simulator may use only the response-type table, not the feature-level model. The effect is the intention-to-treat effect of eligibility for one advertiser's display campaign over one test period (exposure rate among treated users only 3.6%). It does not identify the response to arbitrary ads, prices, content or repeated campaigns, and it says nothing about long-run, carry-over or saturation effects. No timestamps: no time-based holdout, no dynamics, no seasonality; the holdout is user-level (rows are users) only. Near-duplicate feature vectors occur across rows, so held-out users can resemble training users closely. transport() assumes each type's treatment effect is a constant odds ratio when the baseline rate changes; this is an assumption, not an estimate. Visit and conversion are binary per user over the test period; frequency, basket size and revenue are not observed.",
